00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.460
There are layers in a
container, and these layers

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:43.880
have different files
on a file system.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:47.660
So you can think of a container
as kind of like a zip file

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:51.740
with some metadata about what
other layers it depends on.

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:54.500
And so we can build
up a stack of layers

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.560
to have everything that
we need in our container

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:59.750
so that it can run.

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:01.250
So we can see
different layers here.

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:04.190
You can see those
up in the top left,

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:09.290
and we can see the first layer
was only 5.6 megabytes, so

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:11.570
nice and small for
that one, and then

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:14.070
we can go and browse the
files that are in there.

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:17.930
So you can see, this looks like
a basic operating system here,

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:21.140
maybe Ubuntu or Debian
based operating system,

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:22.890
or BusyBox it looks like.

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:26.300
So that was the first
layer, and pretty small.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.310
Then we can see
some other layers

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:29.730
that are added on top of that.

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.360
We add some CA
certificates, then

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:33.860
there's another
layer which looks

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:38.620
like it's adding some
server pieces to it,

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:41.480
and then there's an
index dot html that

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:45.620
gets added in a layer, and
then some SVGs, some assets.

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:49.190
So the whole image size, the
whole container image size

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:51.990
is only 17 megabytes
for this particular one.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:53.780
So that's nice and
small, everything

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:57.800
that's needed to have a portable
container that can run anywhere

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:00.080
that runs containers.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:00.800
OK.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:04.430
So that's our basic
intro to containers,

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:07.250
but now let's talk about
how we create containers.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:11.060
To explore creating containers,
let's go look at an application

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:15.090
that we want to take from
source code to a container.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:18.200
So what we're going to
do is go over to GitHub.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:21.350
I have a repo under my
GitHub, James Warden,

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:24.350
comparing Docker methods, and
there's some different branches

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:25.440
that we're going to
be walking through.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:27.273
The first branch we're
going to walk through

00:05:27.273 --> 00:05:31.290
is the Docker file method, and
we'll open this up in IntelliJ

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:33.370
so that we can explore
it a little better.

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:36.270
I'm going to be using
a Java Maven project

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:38.610
for our explorations here.

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:41.940
But of course, really anything
can be turned into a container

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:42.550
these days.

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:44.550
So all of this stuff
should apply no matter

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:47.020
what technical
technology you're using.

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:50.670
If you're using Node, or
Go, or Python, whatever.

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:53.370
It should be able to be easily
turned into a container,

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:57.040
and we'll have a lot of
similar methods for doing so.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:57.540
OK.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:01.660
So for a first method of turning
something into a container,

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:03.240
we're going to
use a Docker file,

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:05.700
and this is kind of the
traditional standard

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:09.590
for how you take something
and turn it into a container.

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:11.340
Let me first walk you
through this project

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:13.260
so you can get an
idea for the layout.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.060
So this is a Maven project.

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:17.670
So we have our pom
file which defines

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:20.350
how our project is
going to be built,

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:24.040
and then we have our source
code which, in this case,

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:28.050
I'm just using a plain Java
class that's starting up

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.450
a server, and when
there's a request,

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:33.270
it's going to respond
with hello world, so

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:35.920
super simple application.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:38.910
So let's take a look at how
this works for the Docker file

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:40.290
method.

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:43.500
I have a Docker file, and the
first part of the Docker file

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:45.660
is talking about
how this thing is

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:47.670
going to be built
from source code

00:06:47.670 --> 00:06:49.570
into something that can run.

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:53.010
So what I do is I start
from another container,

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:56.370
and I give it a
indicator as builder

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:58.650
which is going to
say this is just

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:00.990
the builder part of
the package and won't

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:03.630
go into my final container.

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:05.520
I set the work
directory to slash app.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:09.180
I copy the contents of my
source into the container,

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:13.980
then I run Maven compile,
and then jar clone and jar,

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:16.740
and that will create the
jar file so that I can run

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:19.270
my application in this case.

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:22.540
So I've got that now
in the as builder part,

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:26.580
but I need to now take that and
put it into my final container.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:30.840
So I do another from, and this
time I give it the base image

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:35.610
that I'm going to use for this,
for my production container

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:36.480
image.

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:40.110
And so I'm using
the open JDK jre,

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:42.060
and then I'm copying
from the builder

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:45.750
that jar file that was created
from Maven compile and jar jar,

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:49.590
and then putting that
on slash server dot jar,

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:51.060
and then I tell it
a command to use

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:52.630
to when the container starts.

00:07:52.630 --> 00:07:55.630
So I say Java minus
jar, that's server jar.

00:07:55.630 --> 00:07:56.130
OK.

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:58.590
So that's a pretty
straightforward way

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:01.770
to go from source
code to the container,

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:04.720
and we can go try this out.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:08.760
So to try it, I'm going
to say docker build,

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:11.880
and then I'm going
to give it a tag.

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:17.370
The tag is either the URL
or the local coordinates

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:18.850
to this thing.

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:22.290
And in this case, I'm not going
to use a remote registry, which

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:23.620
we'll talk about later.

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:28.400
So I'm just going to say that
my tag is going to be-- whoops.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:31.890
Let's copy this and
paste it, and then I'm

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:35.100
going to say colon where
I get a chance to specify

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:37.049
like a version or
something like that.

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:42.299
And in my case, I'm going
to say this is docker file,

00:08:42.299 --> 00:08:45.180
and then I need to tell
Docker what to actually build.

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:47.170
And so I'm going to say
dot, and so it's going

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:49.230
to find the Docker file there.

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:52.380
And now what Docker will
do is it'll go download

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:56.190
if it needs to the different
layers, the Docker container

00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:57.660
layers, so that
it can build this.

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:00.720
You'll see it's downloading
those from, in this case,

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:04.200
Docker Hub is where it's
getting that, adopt open

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:06.570
JDK open JDK 11
from, and now it's

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:08.880
walking through all the
steps in the Docker file,

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:09.960
running the Maven build.

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:14.070
So now because this is running
outside of my local file

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:18.490
system, it doesn't have access
to the Maven local cache.

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:20.220
So it's downloading
all the Maven stuff

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:24.740
that it needs, and it
has the JDK already,

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:28.360
and it needed to download Maven
and then Maven's dependencies.

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:30.990
So it's downloading all
that from Maven central,

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:33.540
and then it's going
to do the compile,

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:36.550
should be able to start
that up in a second.

00:09:36.550 --> 00:09:40.710
And then once it gets compiled,
it'll create the jar file,

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:42.570
and then from that
jar file, it'll

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:44.500
create that final
container image.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:45.000
OK.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:47.370
So it's created the
jar file, and you'll

00:09:47.370 --> 00:09:49.950
see it says it's removing
those intermediate containers

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:53.770
because it didn't need those
any longer for the final image,

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:56.760
and then it's assembling
the final image starting

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:00.000
with the open JDK 11
JRE as the base image,

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.640
and then copying that
server jar file in,

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:04.210
and then setting
up that command.

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:04.710
OK.

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:07.720
So now we can actually
run this thing.

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:12.690
So what I can do is
say, let's docker run.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:16.730
I do need to map the
port like I did before.

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:19.500
Let's map it to
8080, and then I'm

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:22.810
going to tell it that
container image to run.

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:25.950
So let's run that thing
and see how it works look.

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:29.550
Let's go to our local
host 8080, and great.

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:31.240
Our hello world works.

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:31.740
OK.

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:33.720
So we've got this
working locally

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:36.810
and we've got the
container image,

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:39.300
but now let's go
use that dive tool

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:42.030
and take a look at what's
actually in this container

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:44.230
and how it's structured.

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:47.893
So diving in, we'll see
that dive opens it up.

00:10:47.893 --> 00:10:48.810
We can see the layers.

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:53.850
We see a base image that
is our operating system,

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:55.830
and then it's got a
few other pieces here

00:10:55.830 --> 00:10:58.560
that are tweaking some things
in the operating system.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:03.270
It looks like it's installing
some CA certificates and font

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:07.980
config into the container,
and then this 129 meg layer

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:11.160
is probably our
JRE, and then we've

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:14.910
got the final layer which is
copying in our server jar file

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:17.310
and adding that
into the container.

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:19.800
And that one, in our
case, is pretty small.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:23.550
So total here of 229
megs for all the layers

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:25.170
of our application.

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:29.610
Now it's nice that we have this
layering of Docker containers

00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:32.580
because that makes the cache
validation really great

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:34.980
because if I create a new
version of this container that

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:38.550
only changes that final
layer with a new jar file,

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:40.070
then it doesn't
have to recreate,

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:43.290
and in a lot of cases, doesn't
have to pull down those layers

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:44.740
that it already has.

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:49.560
So there's these
sha digests that

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:52.800
make it so that caching works
really well for each layer

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:56.260
that our container
is assembled from.

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:59.760
OK, so really great
caching support

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:02.320
with the layers in Docker.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:02.820
OK.

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:06.840
So that was the first method
for creating a Docker container

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:07.990
with a Docker file.

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:08.490
All right.

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:11.870
Now for the second method
of building containers,

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:14.130
we're going to use
something called jib.

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:17.130
So I'm going to go and
switch my branch in this repo

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:20.490
