00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:38.940
So all we have to do in the case
of Maven is add that plug-in,

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:42.870
and now we can use jib
to build our container.

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:44.340
So the source code
didn't change.

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:45.497
Nothing else changed.

00:12:45.497 --> 00:12:47.580
You'll see that I don't
have a Docker file anymore

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:50.660
because we don't need one
because we're going to use jib,

00:12:50.660 --> 00:12:53.190
and we could do this
for Maven or Gradle,

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:57.400
and I think there's other JVM
based build plugins as well.

00:12:57.400 --> 00:12:57.900
OK.

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:00.510
So to do this,
we're going to call

00:13:00.510 --> 00:13:05.200
Maven with a target
of jib docker build.

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:06.830
So let's go do that here.

00:13:06.830 --> 00:13:07.750
Run that.

00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:10.560
So we're going to first run the
compile then jib docker build,

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:12.602
and we're going to tell
it the image name that we

00:13:12.602 --> 00:13:16.330
want to store which is comparing
docking methods colon and jib.

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:16.900
OK.

00:13:16.900 --> 00:13:19.050
Now jib assembled
in our container,

00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:22.380
and it did that super
fast because jib

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:25.020
doesn't actually use the
typical docker method

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:26.760
to create these containers.

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:31.170
It has its own implementation,
and it's super fast

00:13:31.170 --> 00:13:34.060
and has great caching
support as well.

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:37.515
So let's go do our
dive on this container

00:13:37.515 --> 00:13:42.600
now and take a look at what's
in inside of this container.

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:47.220
So dive in, and we'll see
that our total container

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:49.800
size is a little bit
less this time, 197 megs.

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:53.350
I think because we're probably
using a different base image.

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:56.280
So we've got our base image
with our operating system,

00:13:56.280 --> 00:13:58.440
different pieces the
operating system, and then

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:02.230
the JVM that we're using is
probably that layer there.

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:06.180
And then the jib plugin kicks
in and adds some pieces in,

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:08.415
and it looks like
there's a layer here

00:14:08.415 --> 00:14:10.290
that's used for our
dependencies, which we'll

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:12.930
get into in a minute, and
then the final layer which

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:18.600
is our class file for
our code of this project.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:24.180
So jib did a great job creating
this nice little container

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:24.720
for us.

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:27.930
Now let's run this
thing and make sure

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:29.390
that everything works.

00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:34.270
I can just say let's run
the jib, one of those,

00:14:34.270 --> 00:14:37.420
and let's go refresh to make
sure that our container works.

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:37.920
Yay.

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:38.650
It does.

00:14:38.650 --> 00:14:39.480
OK.

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:42.250
So that's our container
created with jib.

00:14:42.250 --> 00:14:44.638
One of the nice things
that jib does, though, is--

00:14:44.638 --> 00:14:46.680
you may have seen it
there-- it adds a layer four

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:49.360
our dependencies
in our application.

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:54.150
So in this case, my application
doesn't have any dependencies.

00:14:54.150 --> 00:14:56.610
It's just a basic
Java application,

00:14:56.610 --> 00:15:01.680
but let's switch over to
the jib Spring Boot branch,

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:03.700
and then we'll have
some dependencies.

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:07.260
So in the case of Spring Boot,
I have some dependencies,

00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:10.380
in this case, just the
Spring Boot starter WebFlux.

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:13.590
So that is my dependency
with whole bunch

00:15:13.590 --> 00:15:15.840
of other dependencies
underneath it

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:18.730
that I'm going to use in here.

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:26.038
And so if we go back and run
that Maven Docker build again,

00:15:26.038 --> 00:15:27.580
and we'll give this
a different name.

00:15:27.580 --> 00:15:30.550
We'll call it spring
jib dash Spring Boot,

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:32.770
then it's going to
take the dependencies

00:15:32.770 --> 00:15:35.930
and copy those dependencies
into a layer as well.

00:15:35.930 --> 00:15:39.250
And you'll remember that
the caching in Docker

00:15:39.250 --> 00:15:43.040
makes it so that the cache
and validation is really nice.

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:48.340
So let's use dive and
go check this thing out.

00:15:48.340 --> 00:15:49.000
OK.

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:51.380
So looking at this,
we'll see that now there

00:15:51.380 --> 00:15:55.230
is this layer added by
jib which is 18 megs,

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:56.980
and you'll see over
on the right that this

00:15:56.980 --> 00:15:59.050
is all the dependencies
that come in

00:15:59.050 --> 00:16:01.720
from my dependency
on Spring Boot,

00:16:01.720 --> 00:16:03.790
so 18 megs of
dependency that got

00:16:03.790 --> 00:16:07.790
pulled in for that Spring
Boot WebFlux dependency,

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:10.800
and then all the way down
to my particular code that

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:12.380
was added on another layer.

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:14.140
So if we don't change
our dependencies,

00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:17.500
then that layer doesn't
change, which is super nice.

00:16:17.500 --> 00:16:20.110
Only when we change the
dependencies with that layer

00:16:20.110 --> 00:16:23.230
change and only when we change
our application code would

00:16:23.230 --> 00:16:26.350
this top layer change.

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:28.870
So total now, 216
megs with those

00:16:28.870 --> 00:16:33.900
dependencies with most
of that being the JVM.

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:34.480
OK.

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:37.900
So that's the jib
method, and that

00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:41.380
works on any JVM based
build, but let's take

00:16:41.380 --> 00:16:43.910
a look at yet another method.

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:48.620
So I'm going to go switch over
to the build packs branch.

00:16:48.620 --> 00:16:50.410
Let's check out this one.

00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:54.340
So build packs are in
incubating CNCF standard,

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:58.450
and it's a way to be able to
really throw almost any source

00:16:58.450 --> 00:17:04.390
code at a tool, the build packs
tool, and have the build packs

00:17:04.390 --> 00:17:08.200
know how to turn that source
code into a container image.

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:12.160
So there's no plug-in
in my pom for doing

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:15.640
this, nothing special in there.

00:17:15.640 --> 00:17:18.670
There's nothing in my
project, no Docker file,

00:17:18.670 --> 00:17:20.050
and what we're
going to do is run

00:17:20.050 --> 00:17:24.579
a tool called pack that will
turn my code into a container

00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:25.660
image.

00:17:25.660 --> 00:17:31.300
I'm going to be using the
Google Cloud build packs.

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:33.770
So there's different build
packs that you can use,

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:39.760
and if we go to GitHub and go to
the build packs repo on Google

00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:43.210
Cloud platform, what we'll
see is information about

00:17:43.210 --> 00:17:46.142
the Google Cloud build packs,
the ones that we'll be using,

00:17:46.142 --> 00:17:48.100
and the different languages
that are supported.

00:17:48.100 --> 00:17:51.320
Go, Node, Python,
Java, and .NET.

00:17:51.320 --> 00:17:51.890
OK.

00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:55.460
So that means that we can
give it any application built

00:17:55.460 --> 00:17:57.560
with those technologies,
and it should

00:17:57.560 --> 00:17:59.720
be able to turn that
into a container image

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:03.440
without our code or our
build plug-in having

00:18:03.440 --> 00:18:06.890
to have any knowledge of how to
go be turned into a container

00:18:06.890 --> 00:18:08.040
image.

00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:09.230
So let's give this a try.

00:18:09.230 --> 00:18:11.270
I've already installed
this tool called pack.

00:18:11.270 --> 00:18:15.680
You can just download it
from the build pack website,

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:17.450
then you give it
the builder image

00:18:17.450 --> 00:18:20.520
to use, which is also
a Docker container,

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:22.300
and so this is the build pack.

00:18:22.300 --> 00:18:25.070
So the Java build
pack, the Go build pack

00:18:25.070 --> 00:18:26.930
from Google Cloud
Build packs, and then

00:18:26.930 --> 00:18:30.240
we give it the tag that
we're going to use.

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:32.510
So let's run pack
build on this thing

00:18:32.510 --> 00:18:35.900
and turn that source code
into a container image,

00:18:35.900 --> 00:18:41.250
and then we'll take a look
at the container and dive.

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:43.430
There's also a nice
caching in build packs.

00:18:43.430 --> 00:18:47.870
They do a good job of
preserving stuff across builds,

00:18:47.870 --> 00:18:50.520
and you'll see that it
did this restoring phase.

00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:52.760
So first, it actually
did a detecting phase,

00:18:52.760 --> 00:18:55.910
and it detected that, hey,
this is a Java application,

00:18:55.910 --> 00:18:57.830
and it knows how to
build applications,

00:18:57.830 --> 00:19:00.680
so then it restored
from cache our M2 cache,

00:19:00.680 --> 00:19:02.750
so it didn't have to
re-download stuff.

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:06.000
It did the maven clean
package, and then it

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:07.730
exported the layers.

00:19:07.730 --> 00:19:08.240
OK.

00:19:08.240 --> 00:19:12.000
So now we've got our--

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:12.500
let's see.

00:19:12.500 --> 00:19:13.700
That was build back.

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:16.680
Let's go now dive into that one.

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:22.010
So let's run this on
the build pack one,

00:19:22.010 --> 00:19:23.510
and let's try build packs.

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:27.000
I think that's what
it was, plural.

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:27.650
OK.

00:19:27.650 --> 00:19:30.050
So now this is pulling
that into dive,

00:19:30.050 --> 00:19:31.520
and now we can see
the layers that

00:19:31.520 --> 00:19:35.370
were created from build
packs for this application.

00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:38.120
We can see a base image,
which looks like the operating

00:19:38.120 --> 00:19:42.240
system, and then some other
things on top of that,

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:46.160
and then we get down into this
large layer which is probably

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:49.160
the JVM layer,
and then we have I

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:51.500
think a layer for
dependencies and a layer

00:19:51.500 --> 00:19:54.270
for our application,
something along those lines.

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:57.960
So similar layering to
what we saw with the JVM,

00:19:57.960 --> 00:20:00.800
but you'll see
that, in this case,

00:20:00.800 --> 00:20:04.280
the base image is that
it chose were larger

00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:06.410
than the ones that
were chosen by jib.

00:20:06.410 --> 00:20:08.100
You can customize
that if you want,

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:10.310
but the total image
size for this one

00:20:10.310 --> 00:20:13.370
is 436 megabytes, but
really nice that we didn't

00:20:13.370 --> 00:20:15.530
have to have anything
in our project

00:20:15.530 --> 00:20:18.920
that told it how to create a
container image from our source

00:20:18.920 --> 00:20:19.820
code.

00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:20.450
OK.

00:20:20.450 --> 00:20:23.780
And just for the
sake of validation,

00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:31.040
let's go run our docker run,
give it the port 8080 to 8080,

00:20:31.040 --> 00:20:34.790
and run this thing, and make
sure that our application built

00:20:34.790 --> 00:20:36.380
with build packs works.

00:20:36.380 --> 00:20:37.010
Hello world.

00:20:37.010 --> 00:20:37.510
Great.

00:20:37.510 --> 00:20:38.570
It still works.

00:20:38.570 --> 00:20:40.190
OK.

00:20:40.190 --> 00:20:43.550
So that was the
method of using build

00:20:43.550 --> 00:20:49.120
packs to go from our source code
to our Docker container image.

00:20:49.120 --> 00:20:51.700
Now let's check
out. another option

00:20:51.700 --> 00:20:54.960
which is going to use the Docker
file option we saw before,

00:20:54.960 --> 00:20:57.390
but in the case of JVM
projects, can result

00:20:57.390 --> 00:20:59.250
in really small binaries.

00:20:59.250 --> 00:21:02.010
So for this one, I'm
going to switch over

00:21:02.010 --> 00:21:08.580
to a branch called GraalVM, and
for the GraalVM one, what we're

00:21:08.580 --> 00:21:11.550
doing is we're using the
Docker file because we're

00:21:11.550 --> 00:21:14.730
doing some stuff that
we can't do with jib

00:21:14.730 --> 00:21:15.698
or with build packs.

00:21:15.698 --> 00:21:17.240
What we're going to
do is we're going

00:21:17.240 --> 00:21:19.620
to use this thing called
GraalVM to ahead of time

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:22.410
compile our JVM
based application

00:21:22.410 --> 00:21:25.780
into a native executable.

00:21:25.780 --> 00:21:29.820
So I'm giving it the
GraalVM base image there

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:32.190
for our builder, and
then I'm installing

00:21:32.190 --> 00:21:37.110
into here native image, which
is a tool for GraalVM to create

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:40.020
those native images,
and then I'm also

00:21:40.020 --> 00:21:44.010
downloading a library, a
native binding library, that we

00:21:44.010 --> 00:21:46.410
can use so that our
application doesn't have

00:21:46.410 --> 00:21:49.680
any runtime dependencies
on anything,

00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:51.930
not even an operating system.

00:21:51.930 --> 00:21:55.510
And then I'm going to do my
Maven compile, create my jar,

00:21:55.510 --> 00:21:58.110
and now I'm going to run
the native image tool,

00:21:58.110 --> 00:22:00.030
and I'm going to tell
it some parameters

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:02.130
here for when it creates
the native image.

00:22:02.130 --> 00:22:06.270
I'm telling it to use that
MuslC bundle that I downloaded

00:22:06.270 --> 00:22:08.820
earlier and a few other things.

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:13.380
Static is what does
the static binding

00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:15.900
to the native libraries.

00:22:15.900 --> 00:22:16.530
OK.

00:22:16.530 --> 00:22:19.710
Now here's the cool part
is that for final image

00:22:19.710 --> 00:22:24.390
I'm saying from scratch, which
gives me a plain nothing in it

00:22:24.390 --> 00:22:26.940
Docker image, and
now I'm copying

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:29.190
the output of
native image, which

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:31.920
is this binary
executable called webapp,

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:33.930
copying that into the
container, and then

00:22:33.930 --> 00:22:36.420
telling it to run that webapp.

00:22:36.420 --> 00:22:36.990
OK.

00:22:36.990 --> 00:22:39.120
So this is a JVM
based application

00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:41.130
that we're turning into
a native application

00:22:41.130 --> 00:22:48.880
with no built in bindings to
the native code that's needed.

00:22:48.880 --> 00:22:52.830
So let's go run this one
which I'm going to just copy

00:22:52.830 --> 00:22:55.380
this Docker command here.

00:22:55.380 --> 00:22:57.150
Because we're using
a Docker file,

00:22:57.150 --> 00:23:02.200
we're going to do the Docker
build and give it that tag

00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:02.700
there.

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:05.690
OK.

00:23:05.690 --> 00:23:11.550
So now it's doing the builder
stage of the Docker build.

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:16.160
It has downloaded those
native image components

00:23:16.160 --> 00:23:22.110
and the MuslC components, and
now it's doing the Maven build.

00:23:22.110 --> 00:23:24.620
So again, it's going to
have to download the Maven

00:23:24.620 --> 00:23:27.530
stuff for Maven central
so that Maven can run,

00:23:27.530 --> 00:23:29.660
and so that'll take a minute
to download everything

00:23:29.660 --> 00:23:32.570
that it needs, and then
it'll create our jar file--

00:23:32.570 --> 00:23:34.970
or compile the code,
create the jar file,

00:23:34.970 --> 00:23:41.120
and then it will run native
image on that jar file

00:23:41.120 --> 00:23:45.980
to be able to turn it into
that native executable.

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:48.860
So all this takes a
little bit of time.

00:23:48.860 --> 00:23:50.930
And just a
recommendation, no matter

00:23:50.930 --> 00:23:55.490
what method you're using, I
only create my container images,

00:23:55.490 --> 00:23:59.180
usually, as part of
my CI/CD process.

00:23:59.180 --> 00:24:02.660
When I'm developing locally
for JVM applications,

00:24:02.660 --> 00:24:06.500
I'm just using the
standard Java run tooling

00:24:06.500 --> 00:24:09.950
with a JVM installed locally so
I get a really quick feedback

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:14.420
cycle, and I'm not creating
container images as part

00:24:14.420 --> 00:24:16.040
of my local development.

00:24:16.040 --> 00:24:21.320
I just have my CI/CD
server create those images

00:24:21.320 --> 00:24:22.820
because those are
the artifacts that

00:24:22.820 --> 00:24:25.940
are needed to run in
Kubernetes or Cloud Run

00:24:25.940 --> 00:24:29.130
or wherever you want to
run your container images.

00:24:29.130 --> 00:24:29.660
OK.

00:24:29.660 --> 00:24:32.000
So native image is now
running on our jar files

00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:36.680
and has ahead of time
compiled that JVM application

00:24:36.680 --> 00:24:39.890
into a native binary, and
then it has stored that

00:24:39.890 --> 00:24:45.260
into the Docker container, and
now we can run this container,

00:24:45.260 --> 00:24:47.640
and let's make sure it works.

00:24:47.640 --> 00:24:53.200
So let's say run, and
then let's map our port.

00:24:53.200 --> 00:24:53.960
OK.

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:55.050
Run that thing.

00:24:55.050 --> 00:24:57.560
So one of the things that's
really nice about GraalVM

00:24:57.560 --> 00:25:00.650
native images is that they start
up really fast and usually use

00:25:00.650 --> 00:25:01.890
a lot less memory.

00:25:01.890 --> 00:25:04.620
So they work great in
serverless environments

00:25:04.620 --> 00:25:06.030
that benefit from that.

00:25:06.030 --> 00:25:06.530
OK.

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:07.863
Looks like everything's working.

00:25:07.863 --> 00:25:08.840
That's awesome.

00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:12.140
Now let's use our dive
tool, and let's go

00:25:12.140 --> 00:25:16.070
explore our native image
that was created here.

00:25:16.070 --> 00:25:19.550
So now you'll see, because I
did that from scratch, I only

00:25:19.550 --> 00:25:22.220
have one layer in
my Docker container,

00:25:22.220 --> 00:25:25.940
and that one layer only
contains that one file webapp,

00:25:25.940 --> 00:25:27.410
and it's 11 megs.

00:25:27.410 --> 00:25:29.540
So I was able to
have no operating

00:25:29.540 --> 00:25:32.900
system in this container because
all the native bindings are

00:25:32.900 --> 00:25:36.830
built into the executable
that it needs, so super small,

00:25:36.830 --> 00:25:40.370
but this is still
a JVM application,

00:25:40.370 --> 00:25:44.540
just through GraalVM native
image was able to ahead of time

00:25:44.540 --> 00:25:47.900
compile the whole thing down
into a native executable.

00:25:47.900 --> 00:25:50.600
So super cool, and
one thing that you

00:25:50.600 --> 00:25:56.490
can do with containers and
with JVM based applications.

00:25:56.490 --> 00:25:57.320
OK.

00:25:57.320 --> 00:25:59.510
So that was exploring
different ways

00:25:59.510 --> 00:26:03.800
of creating Docker containers
and some of the impacts

00:26:03.800 --> 00:26:06.200
that you have with doing so.

00:26:06.200 --> 00:26:08.930
Now let's learn a little
bit about Docker registries.

00:26:08.930 --> 00:26:10.850
Docker registries
are places that we

00:26:10.850 --> 00:26:12.650
can store these
containers so that they

00:26:12.650 --> 00:26:16.290
can be easily accessed
from different machines.

00:26:16.290 --> 00:26:20.810
So there's a number of
Docker registries out there.

00:26:20.810 --> 00:26:23.630
One of them, the most
typical one that people use

00:26:23.630 --> 00:26:25.610
is just Docker Hub.

00:26:25.610 --> 00:26:28.508
So Docker Hub is
hub.docker.com, and you

00:26:28.508 --> 00:26:30.800
can sign up for an account
and store your containers up

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:32.550
on Docker Hub, and a
lot of the containers

00:26:32.550 --> 00:26:36.020
that we're using as base images,
that's where they come from.

00:26:36.020 --> 00:26:38.360
Now, for your projects
on Google Cloud,

00:26:38.360 --> 00:26:41.480
there's two other places where
you can store your Docker

00:26:41.480 --> 00:26:42.540
containers.

00:26:42.540 --> 00:26:45.830
So let's go over into the Cloud
console and check those out.

00:26:45.830 --> 00:26:49.520
So there's the
container registry

00:26:49.520 --> 00:26:52.358
is the traditional place
to store containers.

00:26:52.358 --> 00:26:54.650
So you can see I've got a
bunch of containers stored up

00:26:54.650 --> 00:26:57.160
on the Google
container registry,

00:26:57.160 --> 00:27:00.830
and you'll remember from
when we did the build pack

00:27:00.830 --> 00:27:03.590
sample that the build
packs are stored up there

00:27:03.590 --> 00:27:06.290
as well, the Cloud Run
hello world is stored

00:27:06.290 --> 00:27:09.440
up there on the Google
container registry.

00:27:09.440 --> 00:27:13.100
There is another place on
Google Cloud which is newer,

00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:16.700
and this is called
the artifact registry.

00:27:16.700 --> 00:27:20.300
The artifact registry is cool
because it supports not just

00:27:20.300 --> 00:27:22.490
Docker containers,
but you can also

00:27:22.490 --> 00:27:26.690
store maven artifacts, NPM
artifacts, that kind of stuff

00:27:26.690 --> 00:27:29.760
up there as well, so not
just Docker containers.

00:27:29.760 --> 00:27:33.090
So you can check that
out, currently in beta.

00:27:33.090 --> 00:27:33.590
OK.

00:27:33.590 --> 00:27:36.590
So let's go back to
the container registry,

00:27:36.590 --> 00:27:39.860
and let's go create
a container that

00:27:39.860 --> 00:27:42.590
will store up on the
Google container registry.

00:27:42.590 --> 00:27:48.500
So I'm going to switch over
to the-- let's go to the jib

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:51.920
branch here and use that one.

00:27:51.920 --> 00:27:53.150
OK.

00:27:53.150 --> 00:27:55.760
Let's go now back
to our command where

00:27:55.760 --> 00:27:58.290
we're going to create
our container image.

00:27:58.290 --> 00:27:58.790
All right.

00:27:58.790 --> 00:28:02.720
So let's copy that command
that we used before

00:28:02.720 --> 00:28:04.730
and paste it in here.

00:28:04.730 --> 00:28:06.430
And then instead of
saying jib docker

00:28:06.430 --> 00:28:10.392
build, I'm going
to say jib build,

00:28:10.392 --> 00:28:12.850
and then I'm going to give it
the coordinates to the Google

00:28:12.850 --> 00:28:13.810
container registry.

00:28:13.810 --> 00:28:16.870
So I'm going to say gcr
dot io slash and then

00:28:16.870 --> 00:28:21.400
my project ID on Google Cloud,
so that's jw demo, and then

00:28:21.400 --> 00:28:22.870
my artifact name.

00:28:22.870 --> 00:28:23.370
OK.

00:28:23.370 --> 00:28:24.740
Let's try to run this.

00:28:24.740 --> 00:28:27.760
And what it should do is
create that container image

00:28:27.760 --> 00:28:31.090
and store it up on the
Google container registry.

00:28:31.090 --> 00:28:34.210
So it looks like it's pushing
those layers up to the Google

00:28:34.210 --> 00:28:35.500
container registry.

00:28:35.500 --> 00:28:39.010
And in this case, it didn't
need the local Docker daemon

00:28:39.010 --> 00:28:42.160
because it didn't actually
store that on the local Docker

00:28:42.160 --> 00:28:42.800
daemon.

00:28:42.800 --> 00:28:43.300
OK.

00:28:43.300 --> 00:28:46.000
Now if we refresh the
Google container registry

00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:50.980
and we come down, we should
see a new Docker container

00:28:50.980 --> 00:28:53.590
image here comparing
Docker methods,

00:28:53.590 --> 00:28:56.373
and then we see that one
that I just pushed there,

00:28:56.373 --> 00:28:58.540
and then I can come and
click on this and say, let's

00:28:58.540 --> 00:29:02.360
deploy it on Cloud Run,
GKE, GCE, or whatever.

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:07.600
So that's how easy it is to
be able to take source code

00:29:07.600 --> 00:29:10.930
and get that container image
up on the Google container

00:29:10.930 --> 00:29:12.790
registry

00:29:12.790 --> 00:29:16.810
Let's go and walk through
one other quick step

00:29:16.810 --> 00:29:20.020
for this which is deploying
this on Cloud Run, which you've

00:29:20.020 --> 00:29:21.520
seen already, but
I want to show you

00:29:21.520 --> 00:29:24.910
what it looks like if we
were to do it from that menu.

00:29:24.910 --> 00:29:27.430
So just like we saw before,
we can create a service.

00:29:27.430 --> 00:29:29.230
We can give it the service name.

00:29:29.230 --> 00:29:32.230
We can tell it, yes,
unauthenticated request,

00:29:32.230 --> 00:29:36.230
the container image URL,
and then let's hit create.

00:29:36.230 --> 00:29:36.730
OK.

00:29:36.730 --> 00:29:40.000
So now it's creating that
service from that source code

00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:44.080
and deploying the new
revision, and that will be up

00:29:44.080 --> 00:29:48.020
and running in just a second.

00:29:48.020 --> 00:29:48.520
OK.

00:29:48.520 --> 00:29:49.020
Great.

00:29:49.020 --> 00:29:51.010
So it's up and running,
and there it is.

00:29:51.010 --> 00:29:54.160
Hello world running
up on the cloud.

00:29:54.160 --> 00:29:57.300
So if you do want to play
around with this stuff,

00:29:57.300 --> 00:30:00.080
